findings.evidence:
roi_stats.py:266-267 and 276:
    if len(verts) == 0:
        raise ValueError("empty ROI vertex set")
    ...
    return p[rows][:, verts].mean(axis=1)
Measured: mask = np.zeros(8, dtype=bool); len(mask) == 8, so the guard is not triggered; shipped returns array([nan]) of shape (1,). A partially-true mask works correctly (mask over [1,2,3] gives 42.0, identical to integer verts [1,2,3]), which is exactly what makes the all-False case dangerous -- masks appear to be a supported input right up until the ROI is empty.

findings.fix:
Normalise verts to integer indices before the guard, and reject booleans explicitly (they are almost always an unintended mask given the documented ROI-as-index-array convention): `v = np.asarray(verts)
if v.dtype == np.bool_:
    raise TypeError("verts must be integer vertex indices, not a boolean mask; pass np.flatnonzero(mask)")
if v.size == 0:
    raise ValueError("empty ROI vertex set")`. Use `.size`, never `len()`, for this check. The same `len(verts) == 0` idiom appears at spatial_z:62, raw_roi_mean:202 and roi_minus_reference:224 and has the same hole.

findings.evidence:
roi_stats.py:271-276:
    if rows.min() < 0 or rows.max() >= p.shape[0]:
        raise IndexError(...)
    return p[rows][:, verts].mean(axis=1)
The row axis is bounds-checked on both sides; the vertex axis is not checked at all. Measured on preds of shape (3, 20484): verts = [-1] returns 20483.0, i.e. exactly preds[0, 20483]. An out-of-range positive index (e.g. 20484) does raise, but only incidentally via NumPy, with a message that does not name verts.
